I decided to focus on heart failure  because of the burden of this condition  so 5 million people in the United States  have heart failure it's actually the  leading cause of hospitalization among  medicare recipients number one cause of  readmission within 60 days of discharge  and the total cost of heart failure over  30 billion dollars per year many of us  in the room who are clinically active  understand the importance of early  readmissions and the pressure on health  systems to reduce these so this issue of  heart failure and heart failure  readmissions in particular is something  that's on everyone's radar screen right  now in developing strategies to reduce  heart failure readmissions has really  become a national priority so that these  are now publicly reported performance  measures and their financial penalties  for 30-day readmissions so  telemonitoring is a promising strategy  that can enable remote monitoring so  that clinicians can intervene early when  there's evidence of clinical  deterioration and the rationale for this  is that many patients with heart failure  may deteriorate over a period of days to  even weeks before requiring  hospitalization and so if only we had  some way to sort of monitor patients  status that might alert clinicians to  early signs and symptoms of  decompensation so that we could avert  the need for hospitalization or even  worse a death before hospitalization can  occur so to get a better handle on this  period of sort of subclinical  deterioration before hospitalization or  what's going on in that window that's  oftentimes invisible to us as clinicians  before patients come into the emergency  room ER to the hospital we did a case  control study I try to keep the  microphone near me as I sort of turn  around here but just let me know if you  can't hear me so day 0 is the day that  our case patients which are shown in the  upper line there with a triangular hatch  marks the case patients were  hospitalized for heart failure on day 0  and we matched these cases to control  patients based on weight and New York  Heart Association sex and we had daily  weights on these patients so this is  information on patients with heart  failure who are  weighing themselves on a daily basis has  came from a telemonitoring company so at  the beginning of the study 45 days you  see all the way to the far left there  were 45 days prior to the time of heart  failure hospitalization the weights on  these patients were very well matched  and then a few days before the heart  failure hospitalization happened in the  case patients you see that the weight  start to diverge and that the case  patients started to put on weight  somewhere between five and seven days  before hospitalization occurred and so  that kind of lended some credence to  this idea that there was a time period a  window where if only we knew about this  weight change and perhaps some other  symptoms that patients may be  experiencing that perhaps the physicians  could implement interventions to avert  that outcome of hospitalization so in  fact there have been a few studies to  look at this question you know ken  providing physicians with information  about patient's clinical status avert  hospitalization that there was a recent  meta-analysis that came out last summer  by the Cochrane group looking at  telephone support and telemonitoring  programs for patients with heart failure  and the authors of this review concluded  that in fact telemonitoring for patients  with heart failure is effective the last  row there shows you the effect for all  cause mortality so it was effective in  reducing all cause mortality with a  hazard ratio of point 66 and all cause  hospitalization the author's went on to  make comments about how patients found  this technology to be very acceptable  and adapted to use of this technology  easily but when we went through and  starting to look at some of the  individual studies that were included in  this meta-analysis we started to  understand that there were a number of  limitations so that many of these  studies were small most the vast  majority of the 25 studies included in  this meta-analysis had fewer than 250  subjects in fact over half of them had  fewer than a hundred almost all of them  were conducted at a single site and  oftentimes the investigators were  testing the effectiveness of what we  call homegrown interventions so the  investigators developed their own  telemonitoring intervention and then  we're the same people who were testing  the effectiveness which of course might  lead to some  there are many strategies to implement  telemonitoring this is really a  promising approach and sort of despite  the limitations of the evidence-based  telemonitoring is something which is  really taken off it's really captured  quite a market share it's being used in  a number of managed care organizations  and also in the VA as well so to kind of  assess this this question and really get  a better handle on you know ken  telemonitoring really improve outcomes  we conducted this clinical trial and our  primary hypothesis was it telemonitoring  will reduce the combined outcome of all  cause readmission and death over six  months so to be eligible for this study  patients had to be hospitalized for  heart failure in the past 30 days and we  selected patients who were recently  hospitalized because that's a  particularly vulnerable group so you  know once patients are hospitalized for  heart failure they have about a fifty  percent chance based on recent medicare  data for either being rehospitalization  we thought that we would have an  opportunity to do some good their  patients had to be at least 18 years of  age able to speak english or spanish  provide informed consent and they had to  have access to a reliable telephone line  not necessarily a landline it could be a  cell phone it could be a neighbor's  phone but they had to be able to make  these phone calls every day so we  established a recruitment network of  cardiology practices across the country  with particular attention to geographic  diversity but also racial and  socio-economic diversity so these were  all cardiology outpatient practices and  these practices were responsible for  screening and enrolling their own  patients into the study so these are the  cardiologists who are responsible for  taking care of the patients these aren't  you know third parties but these are the  patients doctors they had to review and  manage the information that was coming  in from the telemonitoring system on a  daily basis and in this way the  intervention was really firmly embedded  in real-world clinical practice so this  trial was really sort of a strategy  trial testing a strategy of providing  physicians with more information about  their patients and so the showed you  that there were many different companies  and devices to implement telemonitoring  the one that we selected was developed  by a company Farris innovations their  product is called tell assurance and we  selected that not because of any  financial incentives or any ties to that  company in fact we paid for use of that  telemonitoring package but because it's  a device free system and it doesn't  require any reading by patients all of  the questions are just heard over the  phone and so that removes any barriers  that patients might have around literacy  or vision so all of our sites received  support and training from the Yale  coordinating center we went out and  visited each of these practice sites at  the time that they came on to the study  and we several times a year had  conference calls where all of the site  coordinators participated talked about  how it was that they were identifying  patients and rolling patients how were  they screening any challenges that they  had managing the data we paid the site's  slightly more for telemonitoring given  the increased time burden of managing  that information so the telemonitoring  intervention again this consisted of  daily toll free calls to an interactive  voice response system for six months  patients heard questions about their  general health about heart failure  symptoms and about body weight in every  30 days they also rotate it in 2  questions about depressive symptoms from  the phq to so there were predetermined  responses that triggered variances to  flag clinicians attention so you know  for the question of are you having more  shortness of breath in the last day of a  patient answered yes that would trigger  what we call two variants so these  variances are things that might signal  to the clinician that the patient was  getting into trouble so let's dial the  specific questions here just to read  through these quickly to give you a  sense of the real content of this so  compared with yesterday would you say  that you're feeling better the same  worse or much worse have you felt more  short of breath in the last day have you  noticed more swelling in the last day  did you wake up short of breath last  night did you sleep in a chair with more  pillows last night have you had any  dizziness in the last day and then the  depressive symptoms questions are listed  there those are rotated in every 30 days  as I said and then finally patients  enter their weight using a telephone  keypad patients did have to have a  touch-tone telephone we were surprised  at how many patients actually still had  the old rotary phones but we were able  to provide those to patients who needed  them as well so clinicians role in this  was that they had to review the  telemonitoring data every business day  so this meant monday through friday we  weren't expecting physicians to review  this data on weekends or on holidays you  know basically this was sort of part of  their office practice our protocol  required that the clinicians contact  participants whose data indicated  worsening clinical status so those are  the variances that I mentioned earlier  and they actually had to document their  responses right in the telemonitoring  website and i'll show you a screenshot  of what that look like in a minute so  typically this review of the  telemonitoring data was done by nurses  who typically manage this data  independently they could obtain  physician input if they need it  depending on what it was that was going  on with a patient sort of the severity  and patient individual clinical  circumstances but typically these are  pretty high-functioning mid-level  practitioners who oftentimes had  protocols for diuretics and this sort of  thing according to amount of waking or  their symptoms that were going on so  this is a screenshot from the Pharaohs  website and you can see this is  basically what it would look like when  the site coordinator the nurse reviewing  the telemonitoring data would log in in  the morning so they would see a list of  their patients at the top who had  variances so we tried to sort of  organize it in a way that would be  efficient for them to review they listed  exactly what the variance was triggered  by in the next column and then the site  coordinator had to go in and sort of  enter in information about what they did  and then also write a free text note so  similar to what you would expect to  happen in an office around management of  any other sort of clinical situation so  we had a number of strategies to promote  adherence to our protocol so  patience they were told that their  information would be reviewed by the  clinicians who were responsible for  managing their heart failure so they  knew that this is to help your doctors  keep you out of the hospital remember  these are all patients who are recently  hospitalized for heart failure and so  our hope is that they would be highly  incentivized highly motivated to use  this system to sort of be even more  connected with their doctor and help  their doctor take the best possible care  of them if the patient's didn't use the  system for two consecutive days then  they received a system-generated  reminder call just an automated call  from the tell monitoring system after  that if they still didn't use the system  then they got contacted by staff by a  live person from the telemonitoring  company to encourage participation and  these staff members were typically  trained as social workers who were very  well worth versed in behavioral change  and could address the barriers and  challenges that patients might have been  experiencing that might have prevented  them from calling so we had a number of  strategies as well to promote adherence  to the protocol for our clinicians of  course remember these are busy you know  cardiology practices who already have a  very full caseload and now they're using  this telemonitoring sort of on top of  everything else so every two to three  weeks the staff from the coordinating  center reviewed their responses to these  variances and we contacted sites if  there was no documentation of how the  variance was managed in order to ensure  that they were looking at the  information and you know typically it  was a matter if there was no  documentation the website typically it  was a matter of you know the nurse told  us yes yes we saw that but we sort of  just didn't get into the system yet to  document it but we sort of kept a close  eye on that every couple of weeks to  make sure that the information was being  looked at was being acted upon so to  give you a sense of the kinds of things  that clinicians were documenting in the  website is provided a couple of examples  here so this is a nurse who contacted a  patient and her note says that the  patient stated he had been eating smoked  turkey sandwiches along with other  high-salt foods encouraged to watch  dietary intake patient denies any  shortness of breath or dis Mia patient  will call with any change in symptoms  so the baseline characteristics of our  two groups are shown here so we enroll  just over one thousand six hundred forty  patients we had 826 who are randomized  to tell monitoring and 827 who are  randomized to usual care so these are  all their characteristics at baseline  the two groups were very well matched  there were no statistically significant  differences our median age was 61 which  is slightly younger than the average  patient with heart failure and may say  something about the kinds of patients  who are willing to use these sorts of  technologies forty-four percent were  female we had good minority  representation you know NIH requires us  to have at least twenty-five percent  minorities most of our minorities or  black race we had close to forty percent  good socio-economic diversities while a  quarter had less than a high school  education almost thirty percent had an  annual income of less than 10,000 and  you know these were sick patients I've  shown you the most prevalent comorbid  diseases here chronic kidney disease  diabetes and coronary artery disease  that were present in almost half of the  participants you know so these aren't  the sort of clean perfect heart failure  only patients that we oftentimes see in  clinical trials of patients with heart  failure and the two groups were very  similar very well matched also in terms  of their use of medications the heart  failure medications that we would expect  patients to be on so ace inhibitors beta  blockers diuretics into jocks and so you  know in any trial it's important to  understand kind of how much of the dose  was delivered and so we look carefully  at our adherence in many different ways  and and thought a lot about how best to  define adherence and that was really a  challenging question for us so i should  say eighty-six percent of patients who  were randomized to tell a monitoring  made at least one phone call and again  you know how one defines adherence to an  intervention like this isn't totally  clear you know people oftentimes will  talk about sort of the crew rate while  seventy percent of the calls were made  over the six-month period we really felt  that it was important to give a measure  that would give a better sense of what  was happening on a weekly basis since we  understand that heart fail  status may change from week to week even  day to day and so we defined it as  making at least three calls per week  somewhere around half the calls per week  and not surprisingly our adherence was  highest at the beginning of the study so  in the first week ninety percent of  patients met good adherence and by the  final week over fifty-five percent were  adherent so our primary endpoint all  calls readmission or death occurred  exactly equally in the two groups  fifty-two percent we had a number of  secondary endpoints as well all cause  readmission death and heart failure  readmission also occurred exactly  equally in the two groups 47-percent  experienced all cause readmission usual  care forty-nine percent telemonitoring  an 11-percent experienced death in the  two groups we included a number of  secondary endpoints I'll just sort of  scroll through these quickly and  reaching the end of my time here with  you but we looked at number of hospital  days number of readmission we looked at  time to event and we didn't see any  differences in any of these secondary  endpoints and I'll just wrap up by  telling you we looked at a number of  subgroup analyses as well to see well  you know perhaps it's not an effective  intervention if you sort of put it out  there in the universe of heart failure  patients but maybe there's a subgroup we  couldn't find anything we looked at age  sex race ejection fraction New York  heart and we couldn't find anything we  looked at a number of site  characteristics as well which I can  elaborate on so we publish this data and  can just tell you very very quickly one  of the criticisms that has come up is  that we should have used a more  sophisticated form of monitoring  technology that just looking at symptoms  and asking patients to weigh themselves  is sort of sort of outmoded and we  should have looked at more physiologic  parameters we should have had  twenty-four seven Manning of this data  by cardiologists ready to respond and  you know sort of fortuitously this is  exactly the study that was published  several months after ours by German  group they enrolled over 700 patients  with heart failure they had patients who  were not just answering questions and  weighing themselves but had EKG strips  coming in on a daily basis they were  checking blood pressure digital  transmission of body weights and their  call centers were man  24 hours a day seven days a week by  cardiologist who were wearing beepers  and can you imagine getting paid by its  silence wait so they were at the ready  and you know these was a group of  patients who were very good about  following directions they had better  adherence rates than ours and it was a  negative study also so I'll wrap up with  that so in conclusion among patients  recently hospitalized for heart failure  telemonitoring didn't improve outcomes  and our results indicate the importance  of a thorough and independent evaluation  of disease management strategies before  widespread adoption
 

